{"url":"https://www.marchfh.com/obituaries/doris-c-morton","excerpt":"The full published obituary (supplied by Jeff, 2026-08-05) confirms and greatly expands the Find A Grave excerpt. Doris Cynthia Morton was born Nov 3, 1927 in Live Oak, Florida; as a teenager in Daytona Beach she attended Campbell Street High School and was crowned "Miss Campbell Street." She later lived in New York City, fellowshipping at Faith Temple under Bishop Dr. Alvin A. Childs Sr., before relocating to Baltimore in the early 1970s, where she married Bishop Miller Elbin Morton, founder of Eden Light Baptist Church, and became its Head Deaconess. She served as a long-time Board Member of the Eastside Democratic Organization (EDO), focused on minority representation and Black political empowerment in Baltimore City.

CRITICAL CROSS-LINK: the obituary states Doris and Miller "owned and operated two tire businesses, one on Huntington Avenue and the other on Harford Road," later combined into one Harford Road location, "Glide N Ride" (now "Quick Tire Ride") — run by the couple for nearly 40 years. This independently confirms and explains the 1983-84 white-paper finding that the Mortons' 2616 Greenmount mortgages tied the property to "Dot Tire Company" and to holdings on Huntingdon Avenue and Harford Road: Dot Tire Company was the Mortons' own tire business, the direct predecessor of Glide N Ride / Quick Tire Ride. After Miller's death in 2012, Doris ran the tire shop alone for another decade, working daily until age 94 and earning the nickname "The Tire Lady"; in her final years her son-in-law Harry M. Smith assisted.

She was predeceased by her parents, Lorenzo Washington and Stella McCray, and her youngest daughter Linda D. Cruse. She is survived by daughters Fleeter M. Reed and Deborah C. Cruse (m. Harry); sons Claude "Champ" Reed and Everett "Chubby" Cruse; step-son Romeko Morton; grandson Maurice (Belinda) Smith; granddaughter Unissa Cruse-Ferguson; great-grandson Kyle Smith; nephew Corey White; niece "Rosemary"; and several godchildren.","evidence":"Full published obituary text, supplied directly by Jeff Jetton, reviewed 2026-08-05; corroborates findagrave.com/memorial/300191878/doris-cynthia-morton.","headline":"Doris Cynthia "Mama" Morton (1927-2026): full obituary — the tire-business empire behind "Dot Tire Company"","image_id":"fag-300191878-doris-morton","relevance":"The precise, poignant hinge of the property's modern story: the owner's death and the property's foreclosure fall within the same few months in 2026, explaining why 45 years of tax delinquency finally ran its course exactly when it did.","confidence":"documented","source_document":"Obituary of Doris C. Morton, March Funeral Homes (Baltimore, MD), marchfh.com, published on or shortly after Jan 28 2026; supplied directly by Jeff Jetton 2026-08-05"}
